Extending DressCode
*******************

.[perex]
A custom rule for your team, a preset for all your projects, a plugin that shares them as a package, an analysis shared by several rules, or upgrade data that moves the users of your library to its new version: when to choose each of the five ways to extend DressCode, and how to run DressCode from your own tool.


Five Places You Can Extend
==========================

A **rule** is the answer to "I also want to check this". It declares the decisions it enforces (how to name them is in [A Rule in Detail |rule-contract#The Decisions a Rule Enforces]), finds their violations and usually offers a fix. Users then set its decisions just like the decisions of the engine: those of a project's rules in the `project` section, those of a plugin's rules in a section named after the plugin. You can write one in an afternoon: [How to Write Your Own Rule |custom-rule].

A **preset** is the answer to "I want this style on all my projects". It is a NEON file shaped like a configuration, and it carries decisions. It can build on another preset, and you can use it for a whole project or just for a part of it. See [Your Own Preset and Plugin |presets-and-plugins].

A **plugin** is the answer to "I want to share this as a package". It is a class implementing `DressCode\Plugin`, through which a Composer package brings its rules, presets and analyses to a project. It loads only when the project names it in the key `use`, because it brings code that runs on every check. A plugin does not decide how the code looks: the project or a preset writes the decisions. See [Your Own Preset and Plugin |presets-and-plugins].

An **analysis** is the answer to "several rules need this information about a file". It is a plain class, built on top of the tree, that rules ask for, and the engine computes it for them only once. The built-in ones are [NameResolver and Scope |phpsyntax:analyses], `PhpDoc`, `PhpSymbols` with `PhpSignatures` (what PHP itself declares) and `Types` (the types of the code from the PHPStan of the project). See [A Rule in Detail |rule-contract#Analyses].

**Upgrade data** is the answer to "the new version of my library renamed its API, and users should fix their code". These are not rules but NEON files in a directory that a package announces in `extra.dresscode.upgrading-dir`. They fill the maps of the engine, such as `upgrading.api.replacedClasses`, or the maps of plugin rules. See [Replacement Maps |replacement-maps].

What you cannot extend is the parser. It is a separate library, [PhpSyntax |phpsyntax:]; its grammar is PHP and nothing else, and the tree it produces is the same for all rules. That is exactly why rules can be combined without knowing about each other. You also cannot add your own output format: the output is a contract of its own, and if you want to process the results somewhere else, read the JSON from `dresscode check --format json`.

The terms the pages about extending work with, such as node, slot, pass or claim, are explained in the [glossary |how-it-works#Engine and Rule Terms]. The classes of DressCode an extension can rely on between versions are listed on [DressCode Backward Compatibility |compatibility#Public API].


DressCode Inside Your Own Tool
==============================

If you bundle DressCode into your own tool with its own binary, run the whole command-line application, the `DressCode\Console\Application` class, inside your process, and give it the configuration that applies when the project has none of its own:

```php
use DressCode\Config;
use DressCode\Console\Application;

$application = new Application(script: __FILE__, defaultConfig: new Config(use: ['nette'], paths: ['src', 'tests']));
exit($application->run($argv));
```

`script` is your binary: DressCode uses it to start the parallel worker processes and an upgrade run that continues in a new process, so that they get the same default configuration. Everything else (the commands, options, formats and parallel runs) stays the same as with `dresscode`, and `run()` returns the same [exit codes |cli#Exit Codes]. `run()` itself prints the errors that belong to the run to standard error: an invalid call and a bad configuration with exit code `3`, a rule failure, rules in a loop, and environment errors of the `RuntimeException` type, for example a file that cannot be written or a crashed worker process, with exit code `2`. Other exceptions, for example `LogicException` or `Error`, indicate a bug in the program, and `run()` lets them propagate.

